<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhanapala (poet)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. late 10th – early 11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhanapala was a Jain poet and scholar at the Paramara court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakpati-munja">Vakpati Munja</a> and Bhoja. He wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> prose romance Tilakamanjari and the Paiyalacchi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> lexicon completed in 972, which records that the Paramara army had just sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>, the Rashtrakuta capital. His works are a valuable source for the history of the period.</p></div>
